READING LINKAGE FILE FORMAT IN MADELINE
=======================================

- 2002.06.22.ET
- Updated sometime in 2004 by ET

This directory contains one file:

    linkage.ped

linkage.ped
-----------

The BOTTOM part of the linkage.ped file contains data in LINKAGE format.
The TOP part of the linkage.ped file has been edited to contain labels
for Madeline.  These labels are described below:

The first 6 labels correspond to the what Madeline calls
"CORE" fields which are used for pedigree reconstruction.  The labels
used to identify each column are Madeline's default labels -- you can
use other labels, but it's more work to do so.  After each label,
you must tell Madeline the TYPE of the field using a single-letter
identifier:

    C = Character Field
    N = Numeric Field
    G = Genotype Field (i.e., "120/124" -- doesn't occur in LINKAGE files)
    A = Allele Field

Here, then, is how the core fields are identified.  Note that the 
FamilyIDField (FAMID), IndividualIDField (STUDYID), FatherIDField (FATHER),
and MotherIDField (MOTHER) *MUST* be treated as CHARACTER fields, even
if the identifiers are all numbers:

FAMID    C
STUDYID  C
FATHER   C
MOTHER   C
SEX      N
AFFECTED N

After the core fields, paired allele columns contain the genotype data.
Madeline normally deals with "G" genotype columns, not "A" allele columns.
To make Madeline recognize allele columns, you must REPEAT the MARKERNAME
and identify the paired columns as allele columns using the letter "A":

MARKER1  A
MARKER1  A
MARKER2  A
MARKER2  A
MARKER3  A
MARKER3  A
MARKER4  A
MARKER4  A
MARKER5  A
MARKER5  A
MARKER6  A
MARKER6  A

It's your responsibility to make sure your labels are all correct!  Make 
sure you have an even number of allele columns.  Make sure the labels are all
in CAPITALS.  Also make sure that the marker names are spelled identically
with identical capitalization in your map database.

Once everything looks OK, start Madeline and try to RECOGNIZE the data file:

M>recognize "linkage.ped"

The RECOGNIZE command will create a "Madeline file header" file called
"linkage.ped.mfh".  This is the file you actually OPEN in Madeline. 

Before you open the file, notice that the Linkage format uses zeros, "0",
to represent missing identifiers.  For example, the missing parents of
founding individuals are listed with zeros in the data file.

Madeline, however, by default does not consider zeros to be missing values!
You can see that with this command:

M>list CharacterMissingValue
CharacterMissingValue has 5 elements:
CharacterMissingValue[ 1]="."
CharacterMissingValue[ 2]="/"
CharacterMissingValue[ 3]="0/0"
CharacterMissingValue[ 4]="0/ 0"
CharacterMissingValue[ 5]="0/  0"

(You can just type "list cmv" for short).

Zero is, of course, a quite legitimate value for the temperature outdoors, 
a newborn's age, the score in a hockey game, and so on.  It's quite different
from missing.  That's why it is not in the cmv array by default.

However, we can add zero to the array quite easily:

M>CharacterMissingValue[6]="0"

And, having done that, we can now open the linkage file in Madeline:

M>open 'linkage.ped.mfh'
  6. AFFECTED has 3 levels.
Calculating allele frequencies for   7. MARKER1...
Calculating allele frequencies for   8. MARKER2...
Calculating allele frequencies for   9. MARKER3...
Calculating allele frequencies for  10. MARKER4...
Calculating allele frequencies for  11. MARKER5...
Calculating allele frequencies for  12. MARKER6...
